  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 518 AM MDT Fri Oct 30 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nLow level easterly flow will continue to bring low stratus and \npatchy fog for the next 24 to 36 hours, most widespread over \ncentral and north-central Montana. Meanwhile, a closed mid- level \nlow churning over the Pacific NW will maintain a moist \nsouthwesterly flow aloft over the Northern Rockies and will send \nwaves of spotty light precipitation over the forecast area, \nincluding isolated instances of freezing drizzle. The colder \ncentral/north-central areas and the high valleys along the \nMT/ID/WY borders will be most susceptible to trace amounts up to a\nfew hundredths of an inch of ice accumulation. \n\nThe closed low will begin migrating towards the Northern Rockies \nSaturday night into Sunday. This system will be undergoing \nshearing and weakening, but will still bring a period of \nwidespread precipitation Saturday night through early Monday. Most\nof the lower elevation precipitation will fall in liquid form \nSaturday night and most of Sunday, but there may be some lingering\ncolder air for some light freezing drizzle, again mostly along \nthe Hi-Line and the colder higher valleys of far southwest MT. \nH700 temperatures fall to around -5C by Sunday night and will \nbring more efficient accumulating snow over the higher terrain of \nthe southwest. Colder northerly flow may also bring snow over the \ncentral island ranges, especially if a smaller scale vorticity max\nhelps enhance the precipitation as some deterministic models are \nnow highlighting. \n\nEnsembles then favor ridging aloft to bring drier conditions and \nnear average temperatures for much of the upcoming workweek. \nWesterly flow does increase some, but surface winds don\u2019t look to \nbe much of a concern other than some breeziness along the Rocky \nMountain Front and the nearby plains. - RCG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\n...Periods of dense fog through at least Saturday morning...\n\nThe cool and damp low level easterly flow will maintain nearly \nsaturated surface conditions over central and north-central MT for\nthe next 24 to 36 hours. The dense fog advisory was expanded to \ninclude the lower elevations of Judith Basin county and all of \nFergus, with visibilities trending below the half mile mark. \nConditions will marginally improve during the day today, but \ncontinued moist easterly flow looks to bring another round of fog \ntonight into Saturday morning. Most hires models highlight even \nmore widespread fog than the previous couple of nights. Again \ncentral and north-central locations will see the worst conditions,\nbut some southwest valleys also look to see more instances of fog\ntonight into Saturday morning. Impacts will mostly be directly \nrelated from the reduced visibility, but frost deposition and even\nsome freezing drizzle may cause some slippery spots to develop on\narea roadways, particularly the more susceptible bridges and \nelevated surfaces. \n\n...Light mountain snow and lower elevation rain, snow, and some \nmixed precipitation Saturday night through Monday morning...\n\nThe main trough moves through Saturday night through early Monday\nand brings widespread precipitation to much of the forecast area.\nThe overall pattern and NBM probabilities favor the southwest \nmountains south of I90 for the best chance (60 to 80%) for \nsnowfall amounts in excess of 3 inches. The higher terrain along \nthe Continental Divide and the central island ranges will see some\nsnow, but higher totals will be contingent on local vorticity \nmaximums and northerly flow enhancing the precipitation, which is \nnot looking to be fully aligned with the moisture. A thin cold \nlayer may also encourage some spotty freezing drizzle to develop \nalong the Hi-Line and over some of the higher valleys along the \nMT/ID/WY borders Saturday night into Sunday morning, but impacts \nappear to be limited at this time. - RCG",
